    "1784872182c9f7400b": {
      "type": "_blocks",
      "blocks": {
        "ai_gen_block_ca4c513_9DbyBM": {
          "type": "ai_gen_block_ca4c513",
          "settings": {
            "heading": "Frequently Asked Questions",
            "subtitle": "",
            "heading_alignment": "center",
            "question_1": "What is the correct precharge for my accumulator?",
            "answer_1": "<p>Each application has different requirements. Use the Accumulators Inc. online Sizing Calculator (accumulators.com/tool/accumulator-size-calculator) or contact Paquin at 1-800-260-5664 for free application assistance.</p>",
            "question_2": "How often should I check accumulator precharge?",
            "answer_2": "<p>For high-cycle applications, check at least weekly. For emergency standby or pressure-holding systems, check at least monthly. For remote locations, consider electronic permanent-mount pressure monitoring.</p>",
            "question_3": "Bladder vs. piston — which should I choose?",
            "answer_3": "<p>Bladder accumulators offer rapid response, contamination tolerance, simple maintenance, and low-cost replacement parts. Piston accumulators handle higher pressures, larger volumes, and more extreme temperatures. If your compression ratio exceeds 4:1, choose a piston. Our team can assist with selection — call 1-800-260-5664.</p>",
            "question_4": "When should I use a diaphragm accumulator?",
            "answer_4": "<p>Diaphragm accumulators are best for pulsation dampening applications where small volumes are sufficient. They are typically less expensive than bladder or piston types and are ideal for space-constrained mobile and industrial systems.</p>",
            "question_5": "Can Accumulators Inc. products replace competitors' units?",
            "answer_5": "<p>Bladder accumulators and components are typically interchangeable with other manufacturers. Use the Cross Reference Tool at accumulators.com/tool/cross-reference-products or contact Paquin to verify fit for your specific application.</p>",
            "question_6": "When do I need a safety block?",
            "answer_6": "<p>Safety blocks (isolation valves) are recommended for virtually all accumulator installations. They allow single-accumulator removal from manifold racks without shutting down the entire system, and are required for OSHA/NFPA T3.22 compliant maintenance procedures.</p>",
            "question_7": "What thread types are available?",
            "answer_7": "<p>NPT is standard for bladder accumulators; SAE is standard for piston and diaphragm accumulators. Many other thread types are available from stock or as special orders. Contact Paquin to specify.</p>",
            "question_8": "Does Accumulators Inc. make custom accumulators?",
            "answer_8": "<p>Yes. Custom designs are available for special applications and are proprietary to the customer. Clientele includes Fortune 500 companies, government agencies, and the US military. Contact Paquin to discuss custom requirements.</p>",
            "question_9": "",
            "answer_9": "",
            "question_10": "",
            "answer_10": "",
